# top-crim-05-01: Common Intention (10 MCQs: 121 to 130)
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-05-121", "ch-crim-05", "top-crim-05-01", "Which provision of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 codifies the principle of Joint Liability based on Common Intention (Section 34 IPC)?", ["Section 3(5)", "Section 190", "Section 61", "Section 111"], 0, "A", "Section 3(5) BNS replaces Section 34 IPC: when a criminal act is done by several persons in furtherance of the common intention of all, each is liable as if done by him alone.", {"A": "Correct; Section 3(5) BNS corresponds to Section 34 IPC.", "B": "Section 190 BNS is common object (S. 149 IPC).", "C": "Section 61 BNS is conspiracy.", "D": "Section 111 BNS is organised crime."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 3(5) vs IPC Section 34", "Identify BNS provision for Common Intention."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-05-122", "ch-crim-05", "top-crim-05-01", "Does Section 3(5) BNS / Section 34 IPC create a distinct, standalone substantive offence?", ["Yes, it is punishable with 7 years imprisonment", "No; it is a rule of evidence and principle of joint liability, not a substantive offence", "Yes, in murder cases only", "Yes, when 5 or more persons participate"], 1, "B", "Section 34 IPC / Section 3(5) BNS does not create any new offence; it lays down a principle of joint vicarious liability attributing acts done in furtherance of common intention.", {"A": "No separate sentence exists for S. 34.", "B": "Correct; it is a rule of joint liability, not a distinct offence.", "C": "Applies across all offences.", "D": "5+ persons is unlawful assembly."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 3(5)", "Understand the nature of Common Intention."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-05-123", "ch-crim-05", "top-crim-05-01", "In the landmark 'Indus River Case' (Mahbub Shah v. King-Emperor, 1945), the Privy Council established the crucial distinction between:", ["Motive and intention", "Common intention and similar intention", "Preparation and attempt", "Manslaughter and murder"], 1, "B", "Sir Madhavan Nair drew the classic line: common intention implies a pre-arranged plan and prior meeting of minds; similar intention arises independently without pre-concert.", {"A": "Different legal concepts.", "B": "Correct; common intention requires pre-concert; similar intention does not.", "C": "Attempt doctrines.", "D": "Homicide tiers."}, "Hard", "AIR 1945 PC 118", "Distinguish common intention from similar intention."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-05-124", "ch-crim-05", "top-crim-05-01", "In the famous 'Post Office Case' (Barendra Kumar Ghosh v. King-Emperor, 1925), Lord Sumner used which immortal phrase to hold the guard liable for murder?", ["The hand that strikes is the hand of all", "They also serve who only stand and wait", "He who plans the deed executes the deed", "Silence is consent in murder"], 1, "B", "Lord Sumner quoted Milton's phrase: 'They also serve who only stand and wait', holding that the person standing outside to warn of danger actively participated in furtherance of common intention.", {"A": "Informal paraphrase.", "B": "Correct; 'They also serve who only stand and wait'.", "C": "Conspiracy doctrine.", "D": "Consent maxim."}, "Medium", "AIR 1925 PC 1", "Master Barendra Kumar Ghosh participation doctrine."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-05-125", "ch-crim-05", "top-crim-05-01", "Can common intention develop on the spot during the course of the transaction without prior long-term conspiracy?", ["No, it requires days of written planning", "Yes; common intention can develop on the spur of the moment during the commission of the offence", "Only if approved by a gang leader", "Only in riot cases"], 1, "B", "In Kripal Singh v. State of U.P. and Pandurang v. State of Hyderabad, the Supreme Court held that common intention can develop instantaneously on the spot.", {"A": "Prior meeting need not be long-term.", "B": "Correct; can develop on the spot.", "C": "Leader not required.", "D": "Applies to all group acts."}, "Medium", "Pandurang v. State of Hyderabad, AIR 1955 SC 216", "Understand instantaneous development of common intention."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-05-126", "ch-crim-05", "top-crim-05-01", "What is the minimum number of persons required to attract Section 3(5) BNS / Section 34 IPC?", ["One person", "Two persons", "Five persons", "Seven persons"], 1, "B", "The statutory words are 'done by several persons', which requires a minimum of at least two persons acting together.", {"A": "Cannot have common intention alone.", "B": "Correct; minimum two persons.", "C": "Five is for unlawful assembly (S. 189 / 141).", "D": "No seven person rule."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 3(5)", "Recall minimum number for common intention."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-05-127", "ch-crim-05", "top-crim-05-01", "A and B go together armed with knives to rob C. A stands outside the room holding a knife to prevent anyone from entering, while B stabs C to death inside. A is liable for murder under:", ["Section 103 read with Section 3(5) BNS", "Section 105 BNS only", "Simple hurt only", "No liability as A did not stab C"], 0, "A", "Under Barendra Kumar Ghosh and Section 3(5) BNS, physical participation by guarding the door in furtherance of the pre-concerted robbery and violence makes A jointly liable for murder.", {"A": "Correct; joint liability for murder under S. 103 r/w S. 3(5).", "B": "Not reduced to culpable homicide.", "C": "Fatal stabbing occurred.", "D": "Physical presence and guarding constitutes active participation."}, "Easy", "Barendra Kumar Ghosh, AIR 1925 PC 1", "Apply common intention to guarding accomplice."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-05-128", "ch-crim-05", "top-crim-05-01", "What is the essential element that distinguishes Section 3(5) BNS / Section 34 IPC from Section 190 BNS / Section 149 IPC?", ["Section 34 requires active participation; Section 149 requires mere membership of an unlawful assembly", "Section 34 requires 5 persons; Section 149 requires 2", "Section 34 is a distinct offence; Section 149 is not", "Section 34 applies only to property offences"], 0, "A", "Section 34 requires pre-concert and participation in the criminal act; Section 149 requires membership of an unlawful assembly (5+ persons) with a common object, without requiring individual participation in the fatal blow.", {"A": "Correct; active participation vs mere assembly membership.", "B": "Reversed numbers.", "C": "Section 149 creates a distinct offence; Section 34 does not.", "D": "Applies across all offences."}, "Medium", "Nanak Chand v. State of Punjab, AIR 1955 SC 274", "Contrast Section 34 and Section 149."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-05-129", "ch-crim-05", "top-crim-05-01", "In Suresh v. State of U.P. (2001), the Supreme Court affirmed that participation under Section 34 IPC / Section 3(5) BNS:", ["Always requires overt physical assault", "Can be physical, or by moral support, keeping watch, or giving signals, provided there is shared common intention", "Requires firing of a weapon", "Is irrelevant if conspiracy is proved"], 1, "B", "Participation need not be violent assault; standing guard, giving signals, or keeping watch in furtherance of the common design constitutes sufficient participation.", {"A": "Overt violent act not strictly necessary.", "B": "Correct; non-physical or guarding participation suffices.", "C": "Weapon firing not mandatory.", "D": "Participation is the core of Section 34."}, "Hard", "(2001) 3 SCC 673", "Master modes of participation under Section 34 / 3(5)."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-05-130", "ch-crim-05", "top-crim-05-01", "If four persons are charged under Section 302 read with Section 34 IPC / Section 3(5) BNS, and three are acquitted due to lack of evidence, can the sole remaining accused be convicted with the aid of Section 34 / 3(5)?", ["Yes, an individual can have common intention alone", "No, unless it is proved that the sole accused acted jointly with some other unidentified or named persons who shared the common intention", "Yes, automatically", "No, the sole accused must be acquitted of all charges"], 1, "B", "Under settled law, an individual cannot be convicted with the aid of Section 34 alone, unless evidence shows they acted with other persons (even if unidentified).", {"A": "Single person cannot have joint liability alone.", "B": "Correct; requires proof of other participants.", "C": "Cannot convict alone under S. 34.", "D": "Can be convicted substantively for his own acts."}, "Hard", "Prabhu Babaji v. State of Bombay, AIR 1956 SC 51", "Analyze effect of acquittal of co-accused in Section 34."))

# top-crim-05-02: Common Object & Unlawful Assembly (10 MCQs: 131 to 140)
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-05-131", "ch-crim-05", "top-crim-05-02", "Under Section 189 BNS / Section 141 IPC, an assembly of persons is designated an 'Unlawful Assembly' if it consists of at least:", ["Three persons", "Five persons", "Seven persons", "Ten persons"], 1, "B", "Section 189 BNS / Section 141 IPC strictly requires an assembly of five (5) or more persons having one of the five specified common objects.", {"A": "Three is insufficient.", "B": "Correct; minimum five persons.", "C": "Seven is too high.", "D": "Ten is too high."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 189 / IPC Section 141", "Recall minimum membership of unlawful assembly."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-05-132", "ch-crim-05", "top-crim-05-02", "Which of the following is NOT one of the five statutory common objects specified in Section 189 BNS / Section 141 IPC?", ["To overawe by criminal force the Central or State Government or Parliament", "To resist the execution of any law or of any legal process", "To commit any mischief or criminal trespass or other offence", "To hold a peaceful silent prayer gathering in a private home"], 3, "D", "Section 189 BNS / Section 141 IPC specifies 5 unlawful objects: overawing government, resisting legal process, committing offence/mischief/trespass, enforcing right by criminal force, and compelling illegal acts.", {"A": "Statutory object (First).", "B": "Statutory object (Second).", "C": "Statutory object (Third).", "D": "Correct; peaceful private prayer is not an unlawful object."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 189", "Identify statutory common objects."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-05-133", "ch-crim-05", "top-crim-05-02", "Which provision of BNS 2023 replaces Section 149 IPC concerning every member of an unlawful assembly guilty of offence committed in prosecution of common object?", ["Section 3(5)", "Section 190", "Section 191", "Section 194"], 1, "B", "Section 190 BNS corresponds directly to Section 149 IPC (constructive joint liability of unlawful assembly members).", {"A": "Section 3(5) is S. 34 IPC.", "B": "Correct; Section 190 BNS is S. 149 IPC.", "C": "Section 191 BNS is Rioting.", "D": "Section 194 BNS is Affray."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 190 vs IPC Section 149", "Map BNS Section 190 to IPC Section 149."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-05-134", "ch-crim-05", "top-crim-05-02", "What is the crucial two-fold test for constructive liability under Section 190 BNS / Section 149 IPC?", ["Act was done in prosecution of the common object, OR was such as the members knew to be likely to be committed in prosecution of that object", "Act was planned 24 hours in advance", "All members used deadly firearms", "Act was committed in daylight"], 0, "A", "Section 190 BNS / Section 149 IPC has two limbs: (1) offence committed in prosecution of common object; or (2) offence which members knew was likely to be committed.", {"A": "Correct; two statutory limbs of Section 149/190.", "B": "Pre-planning not required.", "C": "Weaponry irrelevant to definition.", "D": "Time of day irrelevant."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 190 / IPC Section 149", "Master the two limbs of Section 149 constructive liability."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-05-135", "ch-crim-05", "top-crim-05-02", "In Mizaji v. State of U.P. (1959), the Supreme Court interpreted the second limb of Section 149 IPC ('knew to be likely') to mean:", ["Mere possibility of crime", "Knowledge that the offence was likely to be committed, which is more than a mere possibility and implies real foresight", "Certainty that murder will occur", "Prior agreement in writing"], 1, "B", "The Supreme Court held that 'knew' means something more than a speculation or possibility; it implies a state of mind where members foresee the offence as a likely incident.", {"A": "Mere possibility insufficient.", "B": "Correct; real foresight of likelihood.", "C": "Certainty not required.", "D": "Writing not required."}, "Hard", "AIR 1959 SC 572", "Apply Mizaji doctrine on knowledge of likelihood."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-05-136", "ch-crim-05", "top-crim-05-02", "What happens when six persons are charged under Section 302 read with Section 149 IPC / Section 190 BNS, and two are acquitted, leaving four persons convicted?", ["The four can still be convicted under Section 149 without any qualifications", "Conviction under Section 149 cannot stand unless it is proved that there were other unidentified persons making the total at least five", "All four must be discharged immediately", "The offence becomes dacoity"], 1, "B", "In Ram Bilas Singh v. State of Bihar, the Supreme Court held that Section 149 requires at least 5 persons; if acquittals reduce the number below 5, S. 149 fails unless others participated.", {"A": "Four persons cannot constitute unlawful assembly.", "B": "Correct; requires proof that total assembly was 5+.", "C": "Can be convicted individually or under S. 34 if common intention proved.", "D": "Not dacoity."}, "Hard", "AIR 1964 SC 259", "Analyze effect of reducing assembly below five persons."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-05-137", "ch-crim-05", "top-crim-05-02", "Under Section 191 BNS / Section 146 IPC, 'Rioting' is defined as:", ["Whenever force or violence is used by an unlawful assembly, or by any member thereof, in prosecution of the common object", "Whenever five persons speak loudly on a public road", "Whenever two persons fight in a cinema hall", "Whenever traffic is blocked by vehicles"], 0, "A", "Section 191 BNS / Section 146 IPC defines rioting: an unlawful assembly using force or violence in prosecution of common object.", {"A": "Correct; force/violence by unlawful assembly.", "B": "Loud speech is not rioting.", "C": "Two persons fighting in public is Affray under S. 194 BNS / S. 159 IPC.", "D": "Traffic blockage is nuisance."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 191 / IPC Section 146", "Define Rioting under BNS."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-05-138", "ch-crim-05", "top-crim-05-02", "Under Section 194 BNS / Section 159 IPC, 'Affray' requires:", ["Five persons using deadly weapons", "Two or more persons, by fighting in a public place, disturbing the public peace", "Damage to private property exceeding Rs 10,000", "Assembly in a private drawing room"], 1, "B", "Section 194 BNS / Section 159 IPC defines Affray: when two or more persons, by fighting in a public place, disturb the public peace.", {"A": "This is rioting/dacoity.", "B": "Correct; 2+ persons fighting in public place disturbing peace.", "C": "Property damage not required.", "D": "Must be a public place."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 194 / IPC Section 159", "Define Affray in criminal law."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-05-139", "ch-crim-05", "top-crim-05-02", "What is the punishment for committing an Affray under Section 194(2) BNS / Section 160 IPC?", ["Imprisonment up to 1 month, or fine up to Rs 1,000, or both", "Rigorous imprisonment up to 3 years", "Life imprisonment", "Community service for 1 year"], 0, "A", "Affray is a petty offence punished with imprisonment extending up to one month, or fine up to Rs 1,000, or both.", {"A": "Correct; max 1 month or fine.", "B": "Too severe.", "C": "Disproportionate.", "D": "Not code term."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 194(2) / IPC Section 160", "Recall punishment for Affray."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-05-140", "ch-crim-05", "top-crim-05-02", "Can an assembly which was lawful when it assembled subsequently become an unlawful assembly?", ["No, character is fixed at the time of gathering", "Yes; under Section 189 Explanation BNS / Section 141 Explanation IPC, an assembly which was not unlawful when it assembled, may subsequently become an unlawful assembly", "Only if police declare it so under Section 144 CrPC", "Only after sunset"], 1, "B", "The statutory explanation explicitly provides: 'An assembly which was not unlawful when it assembled, may subsequently become an unlawful assembly.'", {"A": "Assembly can change character.", "B": "Correct; lawful assembly can turn unlawful.", "C": "Magisterial order not prerequisite.", "D": "Time irrelevant."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 189 Explanation / IPC S. 141 Exp.", "Understand evolution of lawful into unlawful assembly."))

